WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.450 --> 00:00:04.420
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.420 --> 00:00:11.360
This piece of work shows us how to render
a piece of cloth down to the level of fibers.

00:00:11.360 --> 00:00:15.719
This is a difficult problem, because we need
to be able to handle models that are built

00:00:15.719 --> 00:00:19.270
from potentially over a hundred million fiber
curves.

00:00:19.270 --> 00:00:22.310
This technique supports a variety of goodies.

00:00:22.310 --> 00:00:25.550
One - level of detail is possible.

00:00:25.550 --> 00:00:30.870
This means that the closer we get to the cloth,
the more details appear, and that it is possible

00:00:30.870 --> 00:00:35.650
to create a highly optimized algorithm that
doesn't render these details when they are

00:00:35.650 --> 00:00:36.780
not visible.

00:00:36.780 --> 00:00:40.680
This means a huge performance boost if we
are zoomed out.

00:00:40.680 --> 00:00:46.920
Two - optimizations are introduced so that
fiber-level self-shadows are computed in real

00:00:46.920 --> 00:00:50.720
time, which would normally be an extremely
long process.

00:00:50.720 --> 00:00:53.829
Note that we're talking millions of fibers
here!

00:00:53.829 --> 00:00:59.809
And three - the graphical card in your computer
is amazingly effective at computing hundreds

00:00:59.809 --> 00:01:01.220
of things in parallel.

00:01:01.220 --> 00:01:07.270
However, its weak point is data transfer,
at which it is woefully slow, to the point

00:01:07.270 --> 00:01:13.100
that it is often worth recomputing multiple
gigabytes of data right on it just to avoid

00:01:13.100 --> 00:01:15.249
uploading it to its memory again.

00:01:15.249 --> 00:01:19.999
This algorithm generates the fiber curves
directly on the graphical card to minimize

00:01:19.999 --> 00:01:25.219
such data transfers and hence, it maps really
effectively to the graphical card.

00:01:25.219 --> 00:01:31.369
And the result is a remarkable technique that
can render a piece of cloth down to the tiniest

00:01:31.369 --> 00:01:36.279
details, with multiple different kinds of
yarn models, and in real-time.

00:01:36.279 --> 00:01:40.650
What I really like about this piece of work
is that this is not a stepping stone, this

00:01:40.650 --> 00:01:45.579
could be used in many state of the art systems
as-is, right now.

00:01:45.579 --> 00:01:50.770
The authors also made the cloth models available
for easier comparisons in followup research

00:01:50.770 --> 00:01:51.770
works.

00:01:51.770 --> 00:01:54.889
Thanks for watching and for your generous
support, and I'll see you next time!

